+# Correcting a Booked Visit Is In Scope; Cancelling It Is Not
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-5 declined both changing and cancelling a visit once booked. The [2026-08-08 non-goal ADR](2026-08-08-non-goal-deletion-and-visit-amendment.md) confirmed it deliberate. That ADR also set the rule for reopening a row: narrowing it later is a recorded owner decision with its own non-goal ADR.
+
+On 2026-09-29 the owner asked for a booked visit to be correctable. The request came with this decision, quoted from the intake record:
+
+> "Non-goal NG-5 is narrowed: cancelling a booked visit stays out of scope, but correcting its date and description is now in. Record the narrowing the way the project records non-goal changes."
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Keep NG-5 whole.** Rejected by the owner's decision above.
+2. **Retire NG-5.** Rejected: the owner kept cancellation out of scope.
+3. **Narrow NG-5 to cancellation alone** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+A booked visit's date and description can be corrected. The same rules apply as at booking. A correction changes the existing visit and never adds a second one. Cancelling a booked visit stays out of scope. Deleting a visit stays out under NG-4.
+
+The owner also decided that this request adds no visible way into the correction from the owner's record. A visible entry point may come as a later request.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-5 now declines cancellation only. The Non-Goals table and its preamble record the narrowing.
+- The sample no longer treats a booked visit as fixed. It still keeps no lifecycle state: a visit is never marked cancelled, completed, or amended.
+- A correction must pass the booking date rule, so a visit whose date has passed cannot keep that date. The PRD lists this as an open question.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-5
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-5 row.
+- [PRD Visits](../prd.md#req-vis-003) — the correction capability.
